1
00:00:10,190 --> 00:00:15,190
Hi, I'm Bob Emery, and I'm the Radiation Safety Officer for the University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston.

2
00:00:15,190 --> 00:00:21,190
Since we're a comprehensive health science center, we have this unique perspective in that we're evolved from what's called the benchtop to the bedside,

3
00:00:21,190 --> 00:00:26,190
where we actually have scientists that are working on the benchtop examining different issues,

4
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:31,190
and when discoveries are made, those can actually be moved all the way to the clinical applications,

5
00:00:31,190 --> 00:00:35,190
where we can actually apply these to improve the health status of individuals.

6
00:00:35,190 --> 00:00:41,190
The use of radioactive materials in this work is very important because radioactive materials allow scientists the unique ability

7
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:48,190
to actually follow biological systems at the atomic level, and that's a very unique capability that other procedures don't allow us to do.

8
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:53,190
The ability to use radioactive materials allows the scientists to actually see things occur at the atomic level

9
00:00:53,190 --> 00:00:58,190
and actually see how they move through biological systems, and that's a very important capability to possess.

10
00:00:58,190 --> 00:01:06,190
Radioactive materials are also used for examining issues such as cardiovascular disease, neurodegenerative disorders, and diabetes,

11
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:11,190
so a wide variety of diseases are being examined here at the Health Science Center with the use of radioactive materials.

12
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:18,190
There's no doubt that the major advances that have been made in medicine in the past 50 years have certainly been aided by the use of radioactive materials,

13
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:22,190
and we certainly want to be able to continue with that sort of progress in the future as well.

14
00:01:22,190 --> 00:01:28,190
So inherent to this clinical research work with the use of radioactive materials will be the generation of some waste materials,

15
00:01:28,190 --> 00:01:35,190
but these waste materials are things such as rubber gloves and plastic tubing and glassware that's become contaminated

16
00:01:35,190 --> 00:01:38,190
because it was in contact with the radioactive materials.

17
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:44,190
So once these materials are generated, our folks will go and assist the laboratory or clinician personnel by collecting that waste

18
00:01:44,190 --> 00:01:47,190
and bring it down to our dedicated processing room.

19
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:54,190
The materials are then examined and evaluated and then placed into various processing containers, specialized containers,

20
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:56,190
where then they will be encapsulated.

21
00:01:56,190 --> 00:02:01,190
After the waste is collected, our radiation safety specialists will actually monitor that waste

22
00:02:01,190 --> 00:02:07,190
to verify the radioactive content that has been entered on the label by the clinician or the laboratory personnel.

23
00:02:07,190 --> 00:02:13,190
And that's important because we have to make sure we know how much radioactivity is going into that drum or that container.

24
00:02:13,190 --> 00:02:17,190
So once these drums are filled, we seal them up and put certain labels on them,

25
00:02:17,190 --> 00:02:23,190
and then historically we would ship these waste for permanent disposal to a facility in South Carolina.

26
00:02:23,190 --> 00:02:27,190
But as of July 1, 2008, we are no longer able to ship waste to that facility,

27
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:30,190
and so these drums are actually being stored on site.

28
00:02:30,190 --> 00:02:36,190
Certainly safety and security is our top priority, like anyone else who uses radioactive materials as part of their activities,

29
00:02:36,190 --> 00:02:44,190
but our Health Science Center was never designed or intended to be a long-term radioactive waste storage facility.

30
00:02:44,190 --> 00:02:49,190
And so although I can assure individuals that the wastes are secure and safe now,

31
00:02:49,190 --> 00:02:56,190
as this process continues, there is great concern that we need a permanent disposal facility for these wastes

32
00:02:56,190 --> 00:03:00,190
in order to accommodate the research and clinical activities that will be coming down the pike.

33
00:03:00,190 --> 00:03:05,190
Certainly the issues that we're facing here with waste disposal are not limited to us.

34
00:03:05,190 --> 00:03:09,190
The entire Texas Medical Center is facing the same problem, and it's not limited to the Texas Medical Center.

35
00:03:09,190 --> 00:03:14,190
Any modern biomedical research or clinical care facility has the same concerns

36
00:03:14,190 --> 00:03:19,190
because radioactive material is so important to the core missions of these organizations

37
00:03:19,190 --> 00:03:24,190
that they certainly share my concerns that we have to have a safe and permanent long-term solution to this issue.

38
00:03:24,190 --> 00:03:29,190
I think the more the public begins to understand what these wastes truly consist of,

39
00:03:29,190 --> 00:03:32,190
I think there are many misperceptions about what the waste really looks like,

40
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:37,190
and when they begin to understand that the wastes by and large are dry, solid materials

41
00:03:37,190 --> 00:03:44,190
and there are many layers of protections put around these materials before they are placed into a facility,

42
00:03:44,190 --> 00:03:48,190
I think through improved public understanding they'll begin to realize that, in fact,

43
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:52,190
this can be a very viable solution for this disposal problem,

44
00:03:52,190 --> 00:03:55,190
particularly in an era where we have to be not only concerned about safety,

45
00:03:55,190 --> 00:03:58,190
but now security is a big issue because post-9-11,

46
00:03:58,190 --> 00:04:02,190
the issues about security with regard to radioactive materials have been heightened,

47
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:07,190
so it makes a lot more sense, in my opinion, for any radioactive materials and radioactive waste

48
00:04:07,190 --> 00:04:13,190
to be assembled in a singular place that was designed with both security and safety in mind from the beginning.

49
00:04:13,190 --> 00:04:17,190
These buildings at the Health Science Center were designed to do teaching, research and service.

50
00:04:17,190 --> 00:04:21,190
They were not intended to be long-term radioactive waste storage facilities.
